#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# sin

#% text
# `sin(x)` gives the sine of `x`, with `x` in radians.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `sin(q*pi)` is exact for a rational `q` whose denominator is 1, 2, 3, 4 or 6; other exact arguments stay unevaluated.

#% item
# A minus sign is taken out: `sin(-x)` is `-sin(x)`.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values; large arguments are reduced accurately.

#% item
# `sin` is listable: it applies to each element of a list.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
sin(pi/6)
#% output
#= 1/2

#% cell
sin(pi/4)
#% output
#= (1/2)*2^(1/2)

#% text
# A machine number:

#% cell
sin(1.0)
#% output
#= 0.8414709848078965

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
sin([0, pi/2, pi])
#% output
#= [0, 1, 0]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Multiples of `pi` beyond one turn:

#% cell
sin(100pi + pi/6)
#% output
#= 1/2

#% text
# A ball, and 30 digits from an exact argument:

#% cell
sin(1~30)
#% output
#= 0.841470984807896506652502321630

#% cell
n(sin(1), 30)
#% output
#= 0.8414709848078965066525023216302990

#% text
# A complex machine number:

#% cell
sin(1.0 + 2.0*im)
#% output
#= complex(3.165778513216168, 1.959601041421606)

#% text
# A large argument:

#% cell
sin(1e20)
#% output
#= -0.6452512852657808

#% text
# The sine is odd:

#% cell
sin(-x)
#% output
#= -sin(x)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The Pythagorean identity:

#% cell
simplify(sin(x)^2 + cos(x)^2)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# The derivative and an integral:

#% cell
d(sin(x), x)
#% output
#= cos(x)

#% cell
integrate(sin(x)^2, x)
#% output
#= (-1/4)*sin(2*x) + (1/2)*x

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Multiples of `pi` with other denominators stay unevaluated:

#% cell
sin(pi/5)
#% output
#= sin((1/5)*pi)

#% cell
n(sin(pi/5))
#% output
#= 0.5877852522924731

#% text
# A shift by a full period is not removed from a symbolic argument:

#% cell
sin(x + 2pi)
#% output
#= sin(x + 2*pi)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `cos`, `tan`, `asin`, `sinh`, `trig_reduce`, `exp_to_trig`
